"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[48017],{148017:e=>{e.exports='# Feature Limitations on Shared Compute\n\nThese patterns describe features that are architecturally unavailable or restricted on\nStandard and Serverless compute. Unlike API surface issues (which have code\nworkarounds) or bugs (which will be fixed), these are deliberate security and isolation\nboundaries that require different approaches.\n\n## Pattern: Instance profiles and managed identity\n\n**Severity:** P1\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Both\n**DBR Fix Version:** None\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\nimport boto3\n\n# Instance profile-based access (AWS)\ns3 = boto3.client("s3")\ns3.get_object(Bucket="my-bucket", Key="my-key")\n\n# Managed identity-based access (Azure)\nfrom azure.identity import ManagedIdentityCredential\ncredential = ManagedIdentityCredential()\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\nInstance profiles (AWS) and managed identities (Azure) are attached at the cluster VM\nlevel. On shared clusters, the user REPL process and UDFs run in isolated containers\nthat do not have access to the instance metadata service. Instance profiles are only\navailable from init scripts and internally from Spark.\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative\n\n```python\n# For cloud storage access: use UC storage credentials + external locations\ndf = spark.read.parquet(\n    "s3://my-bucket/path/to/data"\n)  # UC handles auth via external location\n\n# For non-storage cloud services: use UC service credentials\n# (e.g., accessing AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault)\n# Configure service credentials in UC and reference them in code.\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- Storage credentials and external locations are the UC-native replacement for instance\n  profiles when accessing S3, ADLS, or GCS.\n- For non-storage AWS/Azure/GCP services, UC service credentials provide a way to\n  obtain temporary credentials scoped to the user.\n- This is a security-by-design limitation of multi-tenant shared clusters.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern: Untrusted JDBC drivers (e.g., Oracle)\n\n**Severity:** P1\n**Affected Compute:** Standard\n**Languages:** Both\n**DBR Fix Version:** 12.2 (with ANY FILE grant)\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\n# Reading from Oracle or other non-trusted JDBC source\ndf = (spark.read\n    .format("jdbc")\n    .option("url", "jdbc:oracle:thin:@host:1521:orcl")\n    .option("dbtable", "my_table")\n    .option("user", "dbuser")\n    .option("password", "dbpass")\n    .load())\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\nOnly a set of trusted JDBC drivers (MySQL, PostgreSQL, Amazon Redshift, Snowflake,\nMicrosoft SQL Server, Azure Synapse, Google BigQuery) are allowed by default on shared\nclusters. Untrusted drivers (like Oracle\'s) cannot guarantee user isolation because\nthey may write temporary data to shared filesystem locations accessible by other users.\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative\n\n```sql\n-- Grant ANY FILE to the user/group that needs untrusted JDBC access\nGRANT ANY FILE TO `user@example.com`;\n```\n\n```python\n# After ANY FILE is granted, the JDBC read works as normal\ndf = (spark.read\n    .format("jdbc")\n    .option("url", "jdbc:oracle:thin:@host:1521:orcl")\n    .option("dbtable", "my_table")\n    .option("user", "dbuser")\n    .option("password", "dbpass")\n    .load())\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- `ANY FILE` permission still enforces ACLs on tables and external locations governed by\n  Unity Catalog. It only relaxes the JDBC driver trust restriction.\n- Non-admin users need a workspace admin to grant `ANY FILE`.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern: Hive UDFs in Scala\n\n**Severity:** P1\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Scala\n**DBR Fix Version:** 14.2 (session-based UDFs)\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```sql\n-- Creating a UDF from a Hive JAR\nCREATE FUNCTION my_udf AS \'com.example.MyHiveUDF\'\nUSING JAR \'dbfs:/path/to/my-udf.jar\';\n\nSELECT my_udf(col1) FROM my_table;\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\nHive UDFs (classes implementing the Hive UDF/GenericUDF interface loaded from JARs)\ncannot be used on shared clusters. The Hive UDF interface allows arbitrary code\nexecution with access to the JVM classloader, which breaks the isolation model.\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative\n\n```scala\n// Option 1: Session-based Scala UDFs (DBR 14.2+)\n// See: https://docs.databricks.com/en/udf/scala.html\nspark.udf.register(\n  "myUdf",\n  (input: String) => input.toUpperCase\n)\nspark.sql("SELECT myUdf(col1) FROM my_table")\n```\n\n```sql\n-- Option 2: UC Python UDFs (persistent, works across all UC compute)\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION my_catalog.my_schema.my_udf(s STRING)\nRETURNS STRING\nLANGUAGE PYTHON\nAS $$ return s.upper() $$;\n\nSELECT my_catalog.my_schema.my_udf(col1) FROM my_table;\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- Session-based Scala UDFs require DBR 14.2 or later.\n- If the UDF needs to be shared across notebooks/jobs, extract it into a separate\n  notebook and `%run` it as the first step.\n- UC Python UDFs are persistent (until explicitly dropped) and work across interactive\n  clusters, job clusters, DLT, and SQL warehouses (Pro/Serverless).\n- UC Scala UDFs (persistent, like UC Python UDFs) are planned but not yet GA.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern: Dynamic views and RLS/column masking on single-user clusters\n\n**Severity:** P1\n**Affected Compute:** Standard (inverse: the workaround needs single-user)\n**Languages:** Both\n**DBR Fix Version:** None\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```sql\n-- Dynamic view with current_user() for row-level security\nCREATE VIEW secure_view AS\nSELECT * FROM sensitive_table\nWHERE region = current_user();\n\n-- Row filter on table\nALTER TABLE sensitive_table\nSET ROW FILTER filter_func ON (region);\n\n-- Column mask\nALTER TABLE sensitive_table\nALTER COLUMN ssn SET MASK mask_ssn;\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\nDynamic views and UC row/column filters work correctly on shared clusters. The problem\nis the inverse: teams that need MLR, MLlib, or GraphFrames must use single-user mode,\nbut single-user clusters do not enforce dynamic views or row-level security / column\nmasking. This creates a security gap for ML workloads that need fine-grained access\ncontrols on sensitive data.\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative\n\n```python\n# Use a single-user cluster with Fine-Grained Access Control (FGAC) enabled.\n# FGAC on single-user clusters is GA from DBR 15.4 and allows row filters\n# and column masks to be enforced on single-user compute.\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- This is not a Spark Connect limitation per se; it is a gap between the security model\n  (which requires shared compute for enforcement) and the feature set (ML libraries\n  require single-user compute).\n- Fine-Grained Access Control (FGAC) on single-user clusters is GA from DBR 15.4.\n  This is the recommended workaround — it allows row filters and column masks to be\n  enforced on single-user (Assigned) compute.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern: MLR, MLlib, and GraphFrames on shared compute\n\n**Severity:** P1\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Both\n**DBR Fix Version:** None (most pyspark.ml algorithms work; only DistributedLDAModel and FPGrowthModel blocked)\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\n# DistributedLDAModel — still requires RDD API, blocked on Serverless/Standard\nfrom pyspark.ml.clustering import LDA\n\nlda = LDA(k=5, maxIter=10)\nmodel = lda.fit(training_df)\ndistributed_model = model.toLocal()  # Fails if underlying model is distributed\n\n# FPGrowthModel — blocked on Serverless/Standard\nfrom pyspark.ml.fpm import FPGrowth\n\nfpGrowth = FPGrowth(minSupport=0.5, minConfidence=0.6)\nmodel = fpGrowth.fit(df)\n```\n\n```python\n# GraphFrames — still requires RDD API\nfrom graphframes import GraphFrame\ng = GraphFrame(vertices_df, edges_df)\nresults = g.pageRank(resetProbability=0.15, maxIter=10)\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\nMost `pyspark.ml` algorithms now work on Standard and Serverless compute. The exceptions\nare `DistributedLDAModel` and `FPGrowthModel`, which still require the RDD API under the\nhood. GraphFrames also requires the RDD API.\n\n- `DistributedLDAModel` and `FPGrowthModel` require a co-located Spark driver and are\n  not available over the Spark Connect gRPC protocol used by Standard and Serverless.\n- `pyspark.ml` in general previously required JVM-side pipeline fitting, model\n  serialization, and optimizer hooks — most of these have been resolved, leaving only\n  the two RDD-dependent models blocked.\n- The legacy `pyspark.mllib` API directly uses RDDs and is completely unsupported.\n- MLR (Machine Learning Runtime) bundles `pyspark.mllib` and therefore also requires\n  single-user mode when using `mllib`-dependent features.\n\n### How to Respond\n\n**When a user asks about MLlib or `pyspark.ml` on a Standard/Serverless cluster,\ncheck the user\'s code for which specific algorithm is being used:**\n\n- If the code uses `DistributedLDAModel`, `FPGrowthModel`, `GraphFrames`, or any\n  `pyspark.mllib` class — explain these require a single-user (Assigned) cluster\n  because the RDD API is not available on Standard/Serverless.\n- For all other `pyspark.ml` algorithms (LogisticRegression, RandomForest,\n  VectorAssembler, Pipeline, etc.) — these work on Standard and Serverless. Do not\n  recommend single-user mode unnecessarily.\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative\n\n```python\n# Most pyspark.ml algorithms work on Standard/Serverless:\nfrom pyspark.ml.feature import VectorAssembler\nfrom pyspark.ml.classification import LogisticRegression\nfrom pyspark.ml import Pipeline\n\nassembler = VectorAssembler(inputCols=["f1", "f2"], outputCol="features")\nlr = LogisticRegression(featuresCol="features", labelCol="label")\npipeline = Pipeline(stages=[assembler, lr])\nmodel = pipeline.fit(training_df)  # Works on Standard/Serverless\n```\n\n```python\n# For DistributedLDAModel / FPGrowthModel / GraphFrames:\n# Workaround — write data from shared cluster, train on single-user cluster\nprepared_df.write.format("delta").saveAsTable(\n    "my_catalog.my_schema.ml_training_data"\n)\n# Then on a single-user (Assigned) cluster:\ntraining_df = spark.read.table("my_catalog.my_schema.ml_training_data")\nmodel = lda.fit(training_df)\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- Most `pyspark.ml` algorithms are supported on Standard and Serverless.\n- `pyspark.mllib` (legacy RDD-based API) is completely unsupported on Standard/Serverless.\n- For streaming use cases with DistributedLDAModel / FPGrowthModel: simultaneously write\n  to both a streaming table (shared cluster) and a static Delta table. The static Delta\n  table can be read from a single-user cluster for ML training. Note this requires\n  maintaining two copies of data, which is not ideal but is the only supported path.\n- Single-user clusters with "Assigned" access mode cannot read streaming tables directly.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern: input_file_name() blocked by Unity Catalog security rule\n\n**Severity:** P1\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Both\n**DBR Fix Version:** None\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import input_file_name\n\ndf = spark.read.parquet("/path/to/data")\ndf = df.withColumn("source_file", input_file_name())\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\n`input_file_name()` is blocked on Unity Catalog-enabled compute (Standard and\nServerless) because UC prevents exposing raw underlying file paths as a security policy.\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative\n\n```python\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import col\n\n# Use _metadata virtual column for file name\ndf = spark.read.parquet("/path/to/data")\ndf = df.withColumn(\n    "RECORD_FILE_NAME", col("_metadata.file_name")\n)\n\n# Or for the full file path\ndf = df.withColumn(\n    "RECORD_FILE_PATH", col("_metadata.file_path")\n)\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- The `_metadata` virtual column is available on all file-based data sources when using\n  `spark.read`.\n- `_metadata.file_name` returns just the filename; `_metadata.file_path` returns the\n  full path.\n- This is a UC-wide limitation, not specific to Spark Connect.\n'}}]);
